1. In `greger-openrouter--handle-reasoning-delta`, we have:
```elisp
(when reasoning-text
  (setf (greger-openrouter-state-current-reasoning-text state)
        (concat (or (greger-openrouter-state-current-reasoning-text state) "")
                reasoning-text))
  (when text-delta-callback
    (funcall text-delta-callback reasoning-text)))
```

So yes, we ARE calling `text-delta-callback` with the reasoning text chunks. This should stream the text to the buffer.

However, let me check if we're calling the `block-start-callback` to insert the `# THINKING` header before streaming starts:

```elisp
(unless (greger-openrouter-state-reasoning-started state)
  (setf (greger-openrouter-state-reasoning-started state) t)
  (when block-start-callback
    (funcall block-start-callback
             `((type . "thinking")
               (thinking . "")
               (signature . "")))))
```

Yes! We ARE calling `block-start-callback` with a THINKING block. This should trigger `greger--append-streaming-content-header` which should insert the `# THINKING` header.

Let me check what `greger--append-streaming-content-header` does for thinking blocks:

```elisp
(defun greger--append-streaming-content-header (state content-block)
  "Append appropriate header for streaming CONTENT-BLOCK to STATE."
  
  (greger--force-code-close state)
  
  (let ((type (alist-get 'type content-block))
        (has-citations (assq 'citations content-block)))
    (cond
     ((and (string= type "text") (not has-citations))
      (greger--append-text state (concat "\n\n" greger-parser-assistant-tag "\n\n")))
     ((string= type "thinking")
      (greger--append-text state (concat "\n\n" greger-parser-thinking-tag "\n\n")))
     (t nil))))
```

I see the mechanism for handling different content types. The code specifically handles thinking blocks by inserting a special thinking tag. When the type is "thinking", it adds `\n\n# THINKING\n\n` to the buffer. This ensures that thinking content is clearly marked and visually distinct.

